1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,440
Six a.m.

2
00:00:01,440 --> 00:00:02,900
The old observatory on the hill.

3
00:00:03,620 --> 00:00:05,940
You're out of breath, you're freezing, and Kai

4
00:00:05,940 --> 00:00:07,240
is already there, eating a sandwich.

5
00:00:07,860 --> 00:00:08,820
You're late, Kai says.

6
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:10,440
She's been waiting.

7
00:00:11,340 --> 00:00:12,100
Who's been waiting?

8
00:00:12,680 --> 00:00:14,320
Something drops out of the clouds and lands

9
00:00:14,320 --> 00:00:14,920
next to you.

10
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:16,500
Hard enough to crack the paving stones.

11
00:00:17,060 --> 00:00:18,340
A girl in a white jacket.

12
00:00:18,900 --> 00:00:20,740
She stands up, brushes off her knees, and

13
00:00:20,740 --> 00:00:21,000
grins.

14
00:00:21,640 --> 00:00:21,980
I'm Nia.

15
00:00:21,980 --> 00:00:22,740
Want to go up?

16
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:24,820
So what if you could fly?

17
00:00:24,820 --> 00:00:26,020
Not in a plane.

18
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:26,700
Just you.

19
00:00:27,860 --> 00:00:28,820
First, the bad news.

20
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:31,040
You're built for walking, not flying.

21
00:00:31,540 --> 00:00:32,780
If you wanted to flap like a bird,

22
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:35,800
scientists estimate you'd need wings about six metres

23
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:36,060
across.

24
00:00:36,640 --> 00:00:38,680
And chest muscles about twice the size of

25
00:00:38,680 --> 00:00:41,480
a professional bodybuilder's, anchored to a big keel

26
00:00:41,480 --> 00:00:42,680
bone you don't have.

27
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:45,100
Birds put a huge chunk of their muscle

28
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:45,580
into flying.

29
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:47,440
You put yours into your legs.

30
00:00:47,980 --> 00:00:49,740
So Nia's power isn't wings.

31
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:51,160
It's just, up.

32
00:00:52,480 --> 00:00:53,460
She grabs your hand.

33
00:00:53,460 --> 00:00:55,140
Your stomach stays on the ground.

34
00:00:55,620 --> 00:00:56,700
The rest of you doesn't.

35
00:00:57,340 --> 00:00:58,640
One hundred metres.

36
00:00:58,640 --> 00:00:59,660
The town looks like a map.

37
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:01,740
One kilometre.

38
00:01:01,740 --> 00:01:02,560
Your fingers go numb.

39
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:04,320
Nia laughs at your face.

40
00:01:05,080 --> 00:01:06,600
Here's something nobody tells you.

41
00:01:06,980 --> 00:01:09,140
The higher you go, the colder it gets.

42
00:01:09,640 --> 00:01:11,420
About six and a half degrees colder for

43
00:01:11,420 --> 00:01:12,900
every kilometre.

44
00:01:12,900 --> 00:01:16,040
Three kilometres up, it's about twenty degrees colder

45
00:01:16,040 --> 00:01:16,760
than on the ground.

46
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:18,620
Your breath comes out as fog.

47
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:20,220
Nia doesn't care.

48
00:01:20,620 --> 00:01:21,480
She's wearing a jacket.

49
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:24,560
You're wearing a hoodie.

50
00:01:24,560 --> 00:01:26,600
Humans have always wanted this.

51
00:01:27,120 --> 00:01:28,920
And some of them got surprisingly close.

52
00:01:29,540 --> 00:01:32,440
In 1979, a cyclist named Bryan Allen pedalled

53
00:01:32,440 --> 00:01:33,800
a plane across the English Channel.

54
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:35,800
Thirty-five kilometres.

55
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:36,820
Under his own power.

56
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:40,020
The plane had wings almost twenty-nine metres wide,

57
00:01:40,380 --> 00:01:41,680
and it weighed about as much as a

58
00:01:41,680 --> 00:01:42,300
big dog.

59
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:45,400
Nine years later, a Greek Olympic cyclist pedalled

60
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:47,060
a plane from Crete to Santorini.

61
00:01:47,060 --> 00:01:49,820
A hundred and fifteen kilometres, in just under

62
00:01:49,820 --> 00:01:50,620
four hours.

63
00:01:50,620 --> 00:01:52,980
A gust of wind snapped its tail, and

64
00:01:52,980 --> 00:01:54,720
it fell into the sea about seven metres

65
00:01:54,720 --> 00:01:55,280
from the beach.

66
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:56,920
It's still the record.

67
00:01:56,920 --> 00:02:00,260
If you want speed, there are wingsuits.

68
00:02:00,260 --> 00:02:01,960
You jump from a cliff and glide.

69
00:02:01,960 --> 00:02:04,080
About two and a half metres forward for

70
00:02:04,080 --> 00:02:06,568
every metre you fall, at around a hundred

71
00:02:06,568 --> 00:02:07,960
and sixty kilometres an hour.

72
00:02:07,960 --> 00:02:09,560
It's not flying up.

73
00:02:09,560 --> 00:02:11,880
It's falling, very stylishly.

74
00:02:11,880 --> 00:02:14,820
And in 2019, a British inventor flew a

75
00:02:14,820 --> 00:02:16,820
suit with small jet engines on his arms

76
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:19,036
along a beach at about a hundred and

77
00:02:19,036 --> 00:02:20,300
thirty-seven kilometres an hour.

78
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:21,480
That's a world record.

79
00:02:21,820 --> 00:02:23,460
And it's about as close to Nia as

80
00:02:23,460 --> 00:02:24,260
anyone has got.

81
00:02:25,680 --> 00:02:27,780
Nia lands you back at the observatory.

82
00:02:28,160 --> 00:02:29,460
Your legs are jelly.

83
00:02:30,300 --> 00:02:32,020
Inside, under the dome, the rest of the

84
00:02:32,020 --> 00:02:33,420
group is waiting around the telescope.

85
00:02:34,200 --> 00:02:35,200
Kai points at the eyepiece.

86
00:02:35,860 --> 00:02:36,020
Look.

87
00:02:36,580 --> 00:02:36,980
You look.

88
00:02:37,580 --> 00:02:39,300
There's a star that's not a star.

89
00:02:39,900 --> 00:02:40,580
It's moving.

90
00:02:41,140 --> 00:02:43,080
And every night, it's a little bit brighter.

91
00:02:43,800 --> 00:02:45,560
That's what's coming, Kai says.

92
00:02:46,480 --> 00:02:49,220
So if you could fly anywhere, where would

93
00:02:49,220 --> 00:02:49,760
you go first?

94
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:51,000
Tell us in the comments.

95
00:02:51,680 --> 00:02:53,920
And subscribe, because next time, you meet the

96
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:56,340
one who can move things without touching them.
